// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// openproduct-gateway (openproduct-independence OX3) — the product's own
// front door. THIS FILE IS A SECURITY CONTROL, same as core's:
// tests/gateway asserts the binding list renders exactly one service binding
// (CORE — inside the product boundary) and no data resource of any kind.
//
// The platform appears here only as PUBLIC URLS in vars — never a binding.
// That is the seam (19-openproduct-seam.md): no service credential in either
// direction, ever.
//
// No @@wiring(...)@@ tokens and no committed resource ids — this worker holds
// no wired resource, so the file is committed directly rather than rendered.
//
// Secrets (delivered by secrets-live from the escrow — never by hand):
//       wrangler secret put LISTING_SEAM_SECRET --env <env>
//   the seam's shared HMAC key, escrow doc integrations/listing-seam/<env>,
//   the same value as membership-worker's and core's.
//
// THIS FILE ALSO CARRIES THE PRODUCT'S ONLY SCHEDULE (openproduct-worldclass
// RS0), and that is not a convenience — it is the difference between a
// working product and an empty one. `src/index.ts`'s `scheduled()` handler
// rebuilds the search index, sweeps the endorsement counts, ranks the launch
// board and cuts the digest. Until RS0 this file declared no `triggers`, so
// that handler had never fired in any environment: `/search` returned nothing
// for a slug linked from the front page, every count rendered `—`, the
// leaderboard was empty and the digest said nothing had launched. Four audit
// findings, one absent block.
//
// So the rule, from WC-1: A SCHEDULED JOB WITH NO SCHEDULE IS AN OUTAGE, NOT
// A SAFE DEFAULT. Every environment that serves traffic declares `triggers`
// explicitly — never by inheriting a top-level block, because the whole
// failure was a schedule nobody could see while reading the environment it
// was missing from. `tests/gateway/src/bindings.test.ts` asserts each named
// environment has one, so this cannot silently regress.
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
{
  "name": "openproduct-gateway",
  "compatibility_date": "2025-05-01",
  // `global_fetch_strictly_public` IS THE SEAM'S URL EDGE WORKING AT ALL.
  //
  // This worker reaches the platform by URL (PLATFORM_API_URL — api-edge's
  // workers.dev host — for the identity read, S2), never by service binding:
  // that is the independence design (OPX-4, OX). But a Worker's global
  // fetch() to a hostname on its OWN zone — and the account's workers.dev
  // subdomain counts — is, by default, "routed to the zone's origin server,
  // ignoring any Workers mapped to the URL" (Cloudflare compatibility flags,
  // `global_fetch_private_origin`). A workers.dev host has no origin server
  // behind it, so the request died before api-edge ever saw it, and every
  // authenticated route answered 503 "Platform unreachable" — on prod and
  // stage, for any token, from the OX3 swap until this flag. With the flag,
  // such a request "loops back to the front door of Cloudflare and is
  // treated like a request from the Internet", which is exactly what a URL
  // edge means. The two OpenNext workers that call api-edge the same way
  // carry it (their adapter emits it); this one did not.
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat", "global_fetch_strictly_public"],
  // Workers Logs (persisted, searchable in the dashboard): the request line
  // and the structured error lines this worker prints are worth nothing if
  // nobody can read them after the fact. Enabled at the default sampling.
  "observability": { "enabled": true },
  // WORKERS CACHING (openproduct-performance OPP4a).
  //
  // Cloudflare checks a cache BEFORE invoking this worker; on a hit the worker
  // never runs. The whole configuration surface is this flag plus the two
  // headers `router.ts`'s public branch sets — `cache-control` and
  // `Cache-Tag`. No zone setting participates: Cache Rules, Page Rules, cache
  // levels and the default cached-file-extensions list have NO EFFECT on a
  // worker's own cache, on this hostname or any other.
  //
  // Three properties this buys that the hand-rolled `caches.default` layer of
  // OPP4 could not, listed in the order they matter here:
  //
  //   REQUEST COLLAPSING  a burst of requests for the same cold key runs this
  //                       worker ONCE per data centre. A launch board's
  //                       traffic is spiky by construction — a listing hits
  //                       the front page and a hundred readers arrive in the
  //                       same second — and OPP4 paid a database read for
  //                       every one of them.
  //   READ-THROUGH        a hit costs no invocation at all, not merely no
  //                       upstream read.
  //   TIERED CACHE        on by default. The Cache API can never have it: it
  //                       is documented as incompatible with tiered caching,
  //                       on every plan.
  //
  // REPEATED IN EVERY ENVIRONMENT BELOW RATHER THAN INHERITED, for the reason
  // this file's header already gives about `triggers`: the failure that cost
  // this product four audit findings was a block nobody could see while
  // reading the environment it was missing from. `bindings.test.ts` asserts
  // each environment declares it.
  //
  // Unlike the Cache API, this works on `workers.dev` too — so dev and stage
  // get the same cache as prod, which is the first time in this epic that a
  // cache could be verified anywhere but production.
  "cache": { "enabled": true },
  // NO `routes` KEY HERE, DELIBERATELY (openproduct-performance OPP3).
  //
  // `api.openproduct.live` reaches this worker as a Worker ROUTE on the
  // openproduct.live zone. OPP3 argued that only a Route keeps the request
  // somewhere a cache works; that reasoning was half wrong (a Custom Domain
  // has a functional Cache API too, and neither shape consults Cache Rules for
  // a worker's own response — see the epic's IMPLEMENTATION-STATUS). What the
  // Route is actually worth is a canonical hostname and `cf-cache-status`
  // observability, and it stays for those.
  //
  // That Route is owned by TERRAFORM (`apps/openproduct/infra/domain`), beside
  // the apex attachment and the zone itself, because a hostname needs a
  // proxied DNS record before a route can match on it and those two must be
  // created together. Declaring it here as well would give one hostname two
  // owners that converge on different schedules — and the failure mode is a
  // deploy silently reclaiming a route Terraform thinks it holds.
  //
  // `workers_dev` stays true below: a web build made before OPP3 still has the
  // workers.dev host baked into its bundle, and that address must keep
  // answering until every environment has rebuilt.
  "main": "src/index.ts",
  "minify": false,
  "vars": {
    "ENVIRONMENT": "local"
  },
  "env": {
    "dev": {
      "vars": {
        "ENVIRONMENT": "dev",
        "PLATFORM_API_URL": "https://api-edge-dev.oruncloud.workers.dev",
        "SITE_ORIGIN": "https://openproduct-web-dev.openproduct.workers.dev",
        // MD1 — "true" only where Cloudflare Image Resizing is enabled on
        // the zone. A zone feature this file cannot see, so it is stated
        // rather than assumed; "false" serves the original at every width.
        "MEDIA_RESIZING": "false"
      },
      // RS0 — every ten minutes. The cadence is set by what a run COSTS,
      // not by what would be freshest: the index rebuild has its own 60s
      // floor (REBUILD_FLOOR_MS) matched to the public document's cache TTL,
      // so a faster cron would spend runs nobody can observe, while ingestion
      // is capped at MAX_PER_RUN = 25 due sources per run against GitHub's
      // unauthenticated 60-requests-per-hour ceiling (GITHUB_TOKEN is
      // optional by design — the unauthenticated read is the degraded mode).
      // Ten minutes keeps search at most ten minutes stale, closes a day
      // boundary within ten minutes of midnight UTC, and stays inside that
      // ceiling at today's corpus size. RS2 splits ingestion from
      // materialisation onto two schedules, which is what makes this safe at
      // RS3's corpus rather than merely safe now.
      // TWO SCHEDULES, TWO KINDS OF WORK (RS2). The expressions are the
      // ones `contracts/sweep-schedule.ts` names, and the gateway's binding
      // test asserts that — a cron this file invents would silently run
      // materialisation instead of what its author meant.
      //
      //   */10 * * * *   MATERIALISATION. Inward only: the search index, the
      //                  endorsement counts, due launches, the boards, the
      //                  defence sweep, the digest. Mandatory wherever the
      //                  product serves traffic; frequency costs only our own
      //                  compute, and its absence is what the 7 Sep audit
      //                  measured.
      //   23 * * * *     INGESTION. Outward, to GitHub. Hourly because that
      //                  is what the unauthenticated 60-requests-per-hour
      //                  ceiling affords at 25 due sources per run. Minute 23
      //                  so the two coincide once an hour rather than every
      //                  run. RS3's corpus needs GITHUB_TOKEN set before this
      //                  cadence can keep up — 25 listings an hour is the
      //                  whole of it otherwise.
      "triggers": { "crons": ["*/10 * * * *", "23 * * * *"] },
      // OPP4a — stated, not inherited. See the top-level block for why.
      "cache": { "enabled": true },
      // CS1 — THE RATE-LIMIT COUNTER, a Durable Object IN THIS WORKER. The
      // limiter's own header planned it ("a Durable Object added to THIS
      // worker — never a platform binding"), and CS-6 holds it to that: no
      // `script_name`, the class is exported from `src/index.ts`, and the
      // binding allowlist names it as the one object this worker holds, the
      // way MD1's bucket is the one R2 binding. One object per bucket key,
      // so the published number on /docs/api is the number enforced rather
      // than a per-isolate approximation of it. A SQLite-backed class, which
      // is the only kind new classes should be; it keeps no storage today
      // (an evicted bucket is a refilled bucket) and costs nothing to have
      // the option. Stated per environment, never inherited, for the reason
      // `triggers` gives above.
      "durable_objects": {
        "bindings": [{ "name": "RATE_LIMIT", "class_name": "RateLimitBucket" }]
      },
      "migrations": [{ "tag": "v1", "new_sqlite_classes": ["RateLimitBucket"] }],
      // OO3 — WHICH BUILD IS ANSWERING. The Worker's own version metadata
      // (the version id and the moment it was uploaded), carried on /health
      // as `build` and shown on /docs/checks, so "is the deploy I am
      // measuring the one that is live" is a read rather than an inference
      // (WC-H), and so the synthetic can hold every per-tick phase to having
      // run after it. Not a data resource and not a platform binding: it is
      // the runtime telling this worker about itself. Named in the
      // allowlist as the one binding of its kind.
      "version_metadata": { "binding": "CF_VERSION_METADATA" },
      "services": [
        {
          "binding": "CORE",
          "service": "openproduct-worker-dev"
        }
      ]
    },
    "stage": {
      "workers_dev": true,
      "vars": {
        "ENVIRONMENT": "stage",
        "PLATFORM_API_URL": "https://api-edge-stage.oruncloud.workers.dev",
        "SITE_ORIGIN": "https://openproduct-web-stage.openproduct.workers.dev",
        // MD1 — "true" only where Cloudflare Image Resizing is enabled on
        // the zone. A zone feature this file cannot see, so it is stated
        // rather than assumed; "false" serves the original at every width.
        "MEDIA_RESIZING": "false"
      },
      // RS0 — every ten minutes. The cadence is set by what a run COSTS,
      // not by what would be freshest: the index rebuild has its own 60s
      // floor (REBUILD_FLOOR_MS) matched to the public document's cache TTL,
      // so a faster cron would spend runs nobody can observe, while ingestion
      // is capped at MAX_PER_RUN = 25 due sources per run against GitHub's
      // unauthenticated 60-requests-per-hour ceiling (GITHUB_TOKEN is
      // optional by design — the unauthenticated read is the degraded mode).
      // Ten minutes keeps search at most ten minutes stale, closes a day
      // boundary within ten minutes of midnight UTC, and stays inside that
      // ceiling at today's corpus size. RS2 splits ingestion from
      // materialisation onto two schedules, which is what makes this safe at
      // RS3's corpus rather than merely safe now.
      // TWO SCHEDULES, TWO KINDS OF WORK (RS2). The expressions are the
      // ones `contracts/sweep-schedule.ts` names, and the gateway's binding
      // test asserts that — a cron this file invents would silently run
      // materialisation instead of what its author meant.
      //
      //   */10 * * * *   MATERIALISATION. Inward only: the search index, the
      //                  endorsement counts, due launches, the boards, the
      //                  defence sweep, the digest. Mandatory wherever the
      //                  product serves traffic; frequency costs only our own
      //                  compute, and its absence is what the 7 Sep audit
      //                  measured.
      //   23 * * * *     INGESTION. Outward, to GitHub. Hourly because that
      //                  is what the unauthenticated 60-requests-per-hour
      //                  ceiling affords at 25 due sources per run. Minute 23
      //                  so the two coincide once an hour rather than every
      //                  run. RS3's corpus needs GITHUB_TOKEN set before this
      //                  cadence can keep up — 25 listings an hour is the
      //                  whole of it otherwise.
      "triggers": { "crons": ["*/10 * * * *", "23 * * * *"] },
      // OPP4a — stated, not inherited. See the top-level block for why.
      "cache": { "enabled": true },
      // CS1 — THE RATE-LIMIT COUNTER, a Durable Object IN THIS WORKER. The
      // limiter's own header planned it ("a Durable Object added to THIS
      // worker — never a platform binding"), and CS-6 holds it to that: no
      // `script_name`, the class is exported from `src/index.ts`, and the
      // binding allowlist names it as the one object this worker holds, the
      // way MD1's bucket is the one R2 binding. One object per bucket key,
      // so the published number on /docs/api is the number enforced rather
      // than a per-isolate approximation of it. A SQLite-backed class, which
      // is the only kind new classes should be; it keeps no storage today
      // (an evicted bucket is a refilled bucket) and costs nothing to have
      // the option. Stated per environment, never inherited, for the reason
      // `triggers` gives above.
      "durable_objects": {
        "bindings": [{ "name": "RATE_LIMIT", "class_name": "RateLimitBucket" }]
      },
      "migrations": [{ "tag": "v1", "new_sqlite_classes": ["RateLimitBucket"] }],
      // OO3 — WHICH BUILD IS ANSWERING. The Worker's own version metadata
      // (the version id and the moment it was uploaded), carried on /health
      // as `build` and shown on /docs/checks, so "is the deploy I am
      // measuring the one that is live" is a read rather than an inference
      // (WC-H), and so the synthetic can hold every per-tick phase to having
      // run after it. Not a data resource and not a platform binding: it is
      // the runtime telling this worker about itself. Named in the
      // allowlist as the one binding of its kind.
      "version_metadata": { "binding": "CF_VERSION_METADATA" },
      // MD1 — openproduct's OWN media bucket (WC-C). The one data resource
      // this worker holds, and the binding allowlist names it as the one
      // exception rather than dropping its assertion (MD-4, OPX-4). NOT
      // bound in dev, like state-worker's ORUN_STATE: the bucket exists
      // where terraform made it, and a binding to a bucket that does not
      // exist is a deploy that fails. Dev answers 503 storage_unavailable.
      "r2_buckets": [
        {
          "binding": "MEDIA",
          "bucket_name": "openproduct-media-stage"
        }
      ],
      "services": [
        {
          "binding": "CORE",
          "service": "openproduct-worker-stage"
        }
      ]
    },
    "prod": {
      "workers_dev": true,
      "vars": {
        "ENVIRONMENT": "prod",
        "PLATFORM_API_URL": "https://api-edge-prod.oruncloud.workers.dev",
        // The new prod site's workers.dev host until the switch (OPN3), when
        // openproduct.live serves this repository's deploy and this returns
        // to "https://openproduct.live". It is the only CORS origin (cors.ts).
        "SITE_ORIGIN": "https://openproduct-web-prod.openproduct.workers.dev",
        // MD1 — "true" only where Cloudflare Image Resizing is enabled on
        // the zone. A zone feature this file cannot see, so it is stated
        // rather than assumed; "false" serves the original at every width.
        "MEDIA_RESIZING": "false"
      },
      // RS0 — every ten minutes. The cadence is set by what a run COSTS,
      // not by what would be freshest: the index rebuild has its own 60s
      // floor (REBUILD_FLOOR_MS) matched to the public document's cache TTL,
      // so a faster cron would spend runs nobody can observe, while ingestion
      // is capped at MAX_PER_RUN = 25 due sources per run against GitHub's
      // unauthenticated 60-requests-per-hour ceiling (GITHUB_TOKEN is
      // optional by design — the unauthenticated read is the degraded mode).
      // Ten minutes keeps search at most ten minutes stale, closes a day
      // boundary within ten minutes of midnight UTC, and stays inside that
      // ceiling at today's corpus size. RS2 splits ingestion from
      // materialisation onto two schedules, which is what makes this safe at
      // RS3's corpus rather than merely safe now.
      // TWO SCHEDULES, TWO KINDS OF WORK (RS2). The expressions are the
      // ones `contracts/sweep-schedule.ts` names, and the gateway's binding
      // test asserts that — a cron this file invents would silently run
      // materialisation instead of what its author meant.
      //
      //   */10 * * * *   MATERIALISATION. Inward only: the search index, the
      //                  endorsement counts, due launches, the boards, the
      //                  defence sweep, the digest. Mandatory wherever the
      //                  product serves traffic; frequency costs only our own
      //                  compute, and its absence is what the 7 Sep audit
      //                  measured.
      //   23 * * * *     INGESTION. Outward, to GitHub. Hourly because that
      //                  is what the unauthenticated 60-requests-per-hour
      //                  ceiling affords at 25 due sources per run. Minute 23
      //                  so the two coincide once an hour rather than every
      //                  run. RS3's corpus needs GITHUB_TOKEN set before this
      //                  cadence can keep up — 25 listings an hour is the
      //                  whole of it otherwise.
      "triggers": { "crons": ["*/10 * * * *", "23 * * * *"] },
      // OPP4a — stated, not inherited. See the top-level block for why.
      "cache": { "enabled": true },
      // CS1 — THE RATE-LIMIT COUNTER, a Durable Object IN THIS WORKER. The
      // limiter's own header planned it ("a Durable Object added to THIS
      // worker — never a platform binding"), and CS-6 holds it to that: no
      // `script_name`, the class is exported from `src/index.ts`, and the
      // binding allowlist names it as the one object this worker holds, the
      // way MD1's bucket is the one R2 binding. One object per bucket key,
      // so the published number on /docs/api is the number enforced rather
      // than a per-isolate approximation of it. A SQLite-backed class, which
      // is the only kind new classes should be; it keeps no storage today
      // (an evicted bucket is a refilled bucket) and costs nothing to have
      // the option. Stated per environment, never inherited, for the reason
      // `triggers` gives above.
      "durable_objects": {
        "bindings": [{ "name": "RATE_LIMIT", "class_name": "RateLimitBucket" }]
      },
      "migrations": [{ "tag": "v1", "new_sqlite_classes": ["RateLimitBucket"] }],
      // OO3 — WHICH BUILD IS ANSWERING. The Worker's own version metadata
      // (the version id and the moment it was uploaded), carried on /health
      // as `build` and shown on /docs/checks, so "is the deploy I am
      // measuring the one that is live" is a read rather than an inference
      // (WC-H), and so the synthetic can hold every per-tick phase to having
      // run after it. Not a data resource and not a platform binding: it is
      // the runtime telling this worker about itself. Named in the
      // allowlist as the one binding of its kind.
      "version_metadata": { "binding": "CF_VERSION_METADATA" },
      // MD1 — openproduct's OWN media bucket (WC-C). The one data resource
      // this worker holds, and the binding allowlist names it as the one
      // exception rather than dropping its assertion (MD-4, OPX-4). NOT
      // bound in dev, like state-worker's ORUN_STATE: the bucket exists
      // where terraform made it, and a binding to a bucket that does not
      // exist is a deploy that fails. Dev answers 503 storage_unavailable.
      "r2_buckets": [
        {
          "binding": "MEDIA",
          "bucket_name": "openproduct-media-prod"
        }
      ],
      "services": [
        {
          "binding": "CORE",
          "service": "openproduct-worker-prod"
        }
      ]
    }
  }
}
